        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"UNPERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"NONE\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The cited texts support darkness, bondage, mortal danger, death, and deliverance by divine presence or light, but they do not establish the entry's distinctive claim that “Shadow of Death” is a technical inverse of a “Shadow of Christ” and denotes a contrary pattern of living. Psalm 23:4 most naturally describes a faithful person passing through deadly danger, not following a sinful pattern. Psalm 107 describes prisoners situated in affliction and darkness, while Isaiah 9 and Luke 1 describe people inhabiting a condition of gloom and death from which light delivers them. Romans 6:23 supplies a valid theological association between sin and death, but it does not use or define the expression. Philippians 2 likewise describes Christ's humiliation and exaltation without defining either shadow or appointed observances. The common contextual reading—deep darkness associated with danger, affliction, death, or spiritual gloom—therefore explains the phrase across the cited passages with fewer inferred links.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The Shadow of Death is the inverse or opposite of the Shadow of Christ: a patterned way of sin, darkness, and bondage that culminates in death.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"A shadow is an image, outline, or boundary of its subject.\",\n    \"The Shadow of Christ is defined partly by appointed times, feasts, assemblies, and a way of living.\",\n    \"The Shadow of Christ leads through death into eternal life.\",\n    \"The wages of the Shadow of Death end specifically in the pit or grave.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [\n    \"That “Shadow of Death” is a technical inverse or opposite of “Shadow of Christ.”\",\n    \"That the expression itself denotes a contrary behavioral pattern of sin rather than a condition or image of deep darkness, affliction, mortal danger, or death.\",\n    \"That the Shadow of Christ is defined by appointed times, feasts, and assemblies; none of the entry's cited excerpts establishes this identification.\",\n    \"That the expression's wages end specifically in “the pit or grave”; Romans 6:23 associates sin with death but does not define the phrase or specify the grave.\"\n  ],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter's broader claim that shadow uniformly means shelter or defense overstates the semantic evidence; shadows can also signify transience, obscurity, gloom, or deathly darkness.\",\n    \"The chapter's argument that Colossians 2 positively requires or protects continued feast and sabbath observance is not asserted by the glossary entry and requires separate evaluation.\",\n    \"The chapter's etymological connection between tselem, image, and tsel, shade, does not by itself establish a uniform symbolic definition.\",\n    \"The chapter's broader positions concerning heaven, Sheol, Gehenna, annihilation, and eternal punishment are outside the glossary entry's scope.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"“Shadow of death” denotes deathly or profound darkness: contextually, it can be the gloom of mortal danger, severe affliction and captivity, the grave or realm of the dead, or spiritual darkness awaiting divine light. It describes a threatening condition or domain rather than a technical anti-Christian pattern of conduct.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"The phrase identifies deepest darkness associated with death, danger, affliction, or the domain of death, with spiritual darkness as a natural prophetic and Christian extension.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The evidence does not identify the phrase as the formal opposite of a Shadow of Christ or as a sinful way of life. Treating it that way changes a contextual image of deathly darkness into a symbolic system supplied by other texts.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Psalm 23:4 places the faithful, divinely accompanied speaker in a valley of deathly darkness or peril. Calling the valley itself a contrary pattern of sin makes the speaker appear to traverse a sinful way and must be softened into external danger, thereby conceding the counter-reading.\",\n    \"Psalm 107:10 and 107:14 directly coordinate the phrase with darkness, affliction, iron bonds, and deliverance. These are conditions in which people sit and from which they are brought out, not necessarily a chosen pattern of conduct.\",\n    \"Isaiah 9:2 and Luke 1:79 employ darkness/light and sitting/dwelling imagery. The arrival of light naturally answers gloom, oppression, hopelessness, and death's dominion without requiring an independently defined Shadow of Christ.\",\n    \"Romans 6:23 supports the general doctrine that sin results in death, but because it never uses the expression, it cannot determine the phrase's lexical or symbolic referent.\",\n    \"Philippians 2:5-9 supports Christ's obedient passage through death to exaltation but mentions neither shadows nor feasts, assemblies, or appointed times.\",\n    \"The wider Job and Amos usage cited in the evidence includes settings of profound gloom, concealment, the dead, and darkness transformed into morning, strongly favoring a darkness/death semantic field.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It creates a coherent theological sequence linking sin, darkness, bondage, death, and deliverance through Christ.\",\n    \"It brings Psalm 107's bondage, Isaiah and Luke's darkness and light, and Romans 6:23's sin-and-death contrast into one redemptive framework.\",\n    \"Its contrast between death and life is compatible with Christian theological application of the phrase.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"It fits the immediate grammar and imagery of walking through, sitting in, dwelling in, and being brought out of the shadow of death.\",\n    \"It explains Psalm 23 without implying that the faithful speaker is following a contrary pattern of sin.\",\n    \"It accommodates mortal peril, captivity, grave imagery, and spiritual darkness as contextual manifestations of one deathly-darkness image.\",\n    \"It does not depend on texts that never use the phrase to construct a technical opposite called the Shadow of Christ.\",\n    \"It remains compatible with the messianic light of Isaiah and Luke while distinguishing that application from the phrase's underlying referent.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [\n    \"Psalm 23:4 presents the righteous speaker as passing through the shadow of death under God's shepherding. The book must redefine its asserted sinful “pattern” as an external peril to fit this text, which abandons its distinctive core identification.\",\n    \"The repeated locative language—people sit or dwell in the shadow of death and are brought out of it—identifies a condition or domain more directly than a contrary way of living.\",\n    \"Uses associated with the land or gates of death and with darkness turned into morning are naturally explained by deathly gloom but not by a behavioral anti-type of Christ without importing an additional symbolic scheme.\"\n  ],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"COUNTER\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Psalm 23:4\",\n    \"Psalm 107:10, 14\",\n    \"Isaiah 9:2\",\n    \"Luke 1:78-79\",\n    \"Romans 6:23\",\n    \"Philippians 2:5-9\",\n    \"Job 10:21-22\",\n    \"Amos 5:8\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"Isaiah 9 and Luke 1 permit a strong spiritual-darkness application, so sin and bondage can legitimately form part of Christian exposition even though they do not establish the entry's technical definition.\",\n    \"The traditional “shadow of death” vocalization and the critical “deep darkness” analysis remain philologically disputed, but both fit the counter-reading better than the proposed Shadow-of-Christ inversion.\",\n    \"The supplied references establish thematic relationships among sin, darkness, bondage, death, and Christ, but the degree to which those relationships warrant a unified typology remains interpretively contestable.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}"
        }
